# IMDb Movies & Directors Database Analysis (SQL)

Relational database analysis of a movies/directors dataset in MySQL — answering 13 business questions using joins, aggregation, and filtering across two linked tables.

**Tech Stack**

MySQL

**Database Schema**

Two tables, joined on director ID:

**directors table**

Column	                    Description
id	                     Unique director ID
name	                    Director's name
gender	               0/2 = Male, 1 = Female
department	        Department the director works in

**movies table**

Column	                            Description
id	                              Unique movie ID
original_title / title	            Movie name
budget / revenue	                  Financials
popularity	                     Popularity score
release_date	                     Release date
vote_average / vote_count	       IMDB rating data
overview / tagline	          Movie description text
director_id	            Foreign key linking to directors.id

Note: credentials for the source database are intentionally excluded from this README.

**Business Questions Answered**
Retrieve all movie records
Retrieve all director records
Count total movies in the database
Look up three named directors (James Cameron, Luc Besson, John Woo)
Find all directors whose name starts with "S"
Count female directors
Find the name of the 10th woman director (alphabetically)
Identify the 3 most popular movies
Identify the 3 most "bankable" movies (highest revenue-to-budget ratio)
Find the highest-rated movie released after Jan 1, 2000
Find the movie(s) directed by Brenda Chapman
Find the director with the most movies to their name
Find the most bankable director overall (aggregate revenue-to-budget ratio)

**SQL Skills Demonstrated**
JOIN (LEFT JOIN) across two related tables
Aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM) combined with GROUP BY
Calculated ratios across joined data (revenue ÷ budget)
Pattern matching (LIKE), set membership (IN), and date filtering
Ranking and top-N queries with ORDER BY / LIMIT / OFFSET

**Files**
Sql_queries.sql — all 13 queries, labeled a–m to match the question list above

